59
00:04:50,640 --> 00:04:51,910
those sort of responses.

60
00:04:51,910 --> 00:04:55,640
So you could argue that ChatGPT
is an example that suggests,

61
00:04:55,640 --> 00:04:56,900
yes, this often can be true.

62
00:04:56,900 --> 00:04:58,870
We can learn a good
enough reward model such

63
00:04:58,870 --> 00:05:01,100
that if we do PPO, at
least a little bit of it,

64
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:05,440
we can actually outperform
the training examples.

65
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:08,150
PPO and DPO does use
a reference policy.

66
00:05:08,150 --> 00:05:11,150
Both of them do.

67
00:05:11,150 --> 00:05:12,733
And this idea will come up.

68
00:05:12,733 --> 00:05:14,150
We've seen it a
few times already,

69
00:05:14,150 --> 00:05:15,660
and it'll continue
to come up today.

70
00:05:15,660 --> 00:05:17,370
This idea of
thinking essentially

71
00:05:17,370 --> 00:05:19,850
of how far can we
extrapolate, or how far can we

72
00:05:19,850 --> 00:05:21,840
interpolate from our data?

73
00:05:21,840 --> 00:05:24,710
And when do we need
to constrain ourselves

74
00:05:24,710 --> 00:05:28,770
to be fairly close either in the
policy space or something else

75
00:05:28,770 --> 00:05:31,520
so that we don't generalize
to parts of the domain

76
00:05:31,520 --> 00:05:33,550
where we might have
really bad performance?

77
00:05:33,550 --> 00:05:35,340
We saw that in
imitation learning.

78
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:36,810
We saw that in DPO.

79
00:05:36,810 --> 00:05:38,010
We've seen that in PPO.

80
00:05:38,010 --> 00:05:40,550
In all of these cases where
we're thinking, given the data

81
00:05:40,550 --> 00:05:44,448
that we have, how can we
generalize as much as possible,

82
00:05:44,448 --> 00:05:45,115
but not further?

83
00:05:47,870 --> 00:05:50,145
All right.

84
00:05:50,145 --> 00:05:54,485
OK, so we're getting into a part
of the class, which is probably

85
00:05:54,485 --> 00:05:56,860
my favorite part of the class,
though I like-- of course,

86
00:05:56,860 --> 00:05:57,310
I'm biased.

87
00:05:57,310 --> 00:05:58,060
I like all of it.

88
00:05:58,060 --> 00:06:01,320
But we've been
talking about learning

89
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:03,540
from past human preferences.

90
00:06:03,540 --> 00:06:05,100
We first saw that
sort of learning

91
00:06:05,100 --> 00:06:06,850
from past human demonstrations.

92
00:06:06,850 --> 00:06:09,153
Then we saw learning from
past human preferences.

93
00:06:09,153 --> 00:06:11,070
And today, we're going
to think just generally

94
00:06:11,070 --> 00:06:12,790
about learning from past data.

95
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:14,490
So that could be
generated by humans,

96
00:06:14,490 --> 00:06:17,263
or it could be generated by
your robot or something else.

97
00:06:17,263 --> 00:06:18,930
And then next time,
we're going to start

98
00:06:18,930 --> 00:06:21,700
talking about fast or
data-efficient learning,

99
00:06:21,700 --> 00:06:24,240
and that's going to be useful
for doing homework 3 as

100
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:26,460
well, because the theory
question for homework 3

101
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:29,590
is focused on
data-efficient learning.

102
00:06:29,590 --> 00:06:32,470
All right, so we'll
focus on that now.

103
00:06:32,470 --> 00:06:34,840
So in particular, for today,
we're going to discuss,

104
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:38,010
like we often do, thinking of
separating things into a policy

105
00:06:38,010 --> 00:06:40,530
evaluation question
and then a policy

106
00:06:40,530 --> 00:06:43,110
learning question, because
we've seen repeatedly

107
00:06:43,110 --> 00:06:45,150
that if we think
about can we evaluate

108
00:06:45,150 --> 00:06:48,030
how good a particular policy
is, that we can often combine

109
00:06:48,030 --> 00:06:51,400
that as a way to bootstrap
improving our policy

110
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:53,290
optimization.

111
00:06:53,290 --> 00:06:53,790
All right.

112
00:06:53,790 --> 00:06:57,360
But I want to start with just
a question, which is, can we do

113
00:06:57,360 --> 00:06:58,690
better than imitation learning?

114
00:06:58,690 --> 00:07:00,482
And of course, this
relates to the question

115
00:07:00,482 --> 00:07:02,690
I just asked you in the
refresh your understanding.

116
00:07:02,690 --> 00:07:05,160
So I'm just going to give
up sort of an example.

117
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:08,070
In my lab, we often think about
education data or health care

118
00:07:08,070 --> 00:07:11,250
data or other cases where
decisions are being generated

119
00:07:11,250 --> 00:07:15,190
by humans or automated
systems where you might have,

120
00:07:15,190 --> 00:07:16,450
say, a series of patients.

121
00:07:16,450 --> 00:07:19,110
You could think of this
as medical record data.

122
00:07:19,110 --> 00:07:21,120
And each of those
people are getting

123
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:22,570
a series of interventions.

124
00:07:22,570 --> 00:07:23,710
Maybe it's some medication.

125
00:07:23,710 --> 00:07:25,120
Maybe it's a medical checkup.

126
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:26,680
Maybe it's a vaccine.

127
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:30,150
And then we observe
some sort of outcome.

128
00:07:30,150 --> 00:07:33,700
And in imitation learning, we
saw the idea of saying, well,

129
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:35,400
could we try to
mimic the best human,

130
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:37,980
or could we try to
mimic expert data?

131
00:07:37,980 --> 00:07:40,200
And so an important
question is whether or not

132
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:41,880
we can go beyond that.

133
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:43,680
And we just thought
about one example

134
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:45,400
where we might be able
to go beyond that.

135
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:47,490
But I think that there's
a huge number of places

136
00:07:47,490 --> 00:07:50,400
we'd love to be able to go
beyond the limits of at least

137
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:52,720
the average human performance.

138
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:54,370
Health care is
certainly one of them.

139
00:07:54,370 --> 00:07:56,400
In America, we pay a
lot for our health care,

140
00:07:56,400 --> 00:07:58,650
and we don't have particularly
good outcomes compared

141
00:07:58,650 --> 00:08:00,078
to how much we are paying.

142
00:08:00,078 --> 00:08:01,620
So you would hope
that maybe we could

143
00:08:01,620 --> 00:08:04,600
learn through reinforcement
learning or others.

144
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:06,480
Are there better
sequences of decisions

145
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:10,910
we could make in order to better
assist, say, a new patient?

146
00:08:10,910 --> 00:08:17,000
So I'll just give a little bit
of backstory of why I started

147
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:18,390
thinking about this question.

148
00:08:18,390 --> 00:08:22,160
So about a decade ago,
I was collaborating

149
00:08:22,160 --> 00:08:25,410
with Zoran Popovic and his
lab and my grad students.

150
00:08:25,410 --> 00:08:26,940
He's at University
of Washington,

151
00:08:26,940 --> 00:08:29,180
and he had this game
called Refraction.

152
00:08:29,180 --> 00:08:32,900
And Refraction helps teach
kids about fractions.

153
00:08:32,900 --> 00:08:34,669
It's one of the
concepts kids typically

154
00:08:34,669 --> 00:08:38,610
find really challenging when
they start to learn math.

155
00:08:38,610 --> 00:08:40,640
And so in it, you
have a spaceship,

156
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:42,830
and you're trying to fuel
a spaceship by splitting

157
00:08:42,830 --> 00:08:45,030
laser beams in certain ways.

158
00:08:45,030 --> 00:08:47,090
So that you create fractions
or subparts of laser

159
00:08:47,090 --> 00:08:52,370
beams to fuel spaceships and
to save the-- save the agents.

160
00:08:52,370 --> 00:08:56,090
And in this case, so I think
roughly around maybe 500,000

161
00:08:56,090 --> 00:08:57,720
kids have played this game.

162
00:08:57,720 --> 00:08:59,720
And what we were thinking
about is, how could we

163
00:08:59,720 --> 00:09:02,840
customize it to make it more
personalized and adaptive

164
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:03,950
to students?

165
00:09:03,950 --> 00:09:06,470
So in particular, there are
all these different sort

166
00:09:06,470 --> 00:09:08,760
of game activities
and game levels.

167
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:12,900
And we wanted to understand,
how could we use information

168
00:09:12,900 --> 00:09:15,900
about how the student was
working in one of the activities

169
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:19,410
to adaptively select
which next activity to do?

170
00:09:19,410 --> 00:09:23,250
So this is a decision policy
and you can imagine conditioning

171
00:09:23,250 --> 00:09:25,300
on all sorts of state features.

172
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:27,847
So state features could be
like how long they took.

173
00:09:27,847 --> 00:09:30,180
But it also could be things
like where did they put down

174
00:09:30,180 --> 00:09:32,280
laser beams, or what
series of mistakes

175
00:09:32,280 --> 00:09:33,725
did they make, you imagine?

176
00:09:33,725 --> 00:09:35,850
It could be generally a
really, really rich context

177
00:09:35,850 --> 00:09:36,868
or state space.

178
00:09:36,868 --> 00:09:38,910
And then there were lots
of different next levels

179
00:09:38,910 --> 00:09:40,530
we could do.

180
00:09:40,530 --> 00:09:43,290
So that was the question
we were interested in.

181
00:09:43,290 --> 00:09:47,040
And in particular, in this case,
we had access to about 11,000

182
00:09:47,040 --> 00:09:51,660
learners who had been giving
activities in a random order.

183
00:09:51,660 --> 00:09:54,960
Now, that was because there
was a human designer who

184
00:09:54,960 --> 00:09:57,760
had designed a specific
sequence through the game,

185
00:09:57,760 --> 00:10:00,000
but we weren't sure
if that was actually

186
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,370
optimal or close to optimal.

187
00:10:02,370 --> 00:10:04,440
And what we wanted to do
is to see whether or not

188
00:10:04,440 --> 00:10:06,210
we could find
using reinforcement

189
00:10:06,210 --> 00:10:08,580
learning an adaptive
policy to help students

190
00:10:08,580 --> 00:10:10,650
persist at the game for longer.

191
00:10:10,650 --> 00:10:12,270
So this game was
offered on something

192
00:10:12,270 --> 00:10:15,420
called BrainPOP, which some of
you guys might have seen before.

193
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:18,210
It offers lots of
educational games for kids.

194
00:10:18,210 --> 00:10:20,940
And a lot of kids use
it for a little while,

195
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:21,970
and then they stop.

196
00:10:21,970 --> 00:10:23,440
So it's an optional game.

197
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:25,470
And we had some
evidence that suggested

198
00:10:25,470 --> 00:10:27,450
that if kids played
the game, they

199
00:10:27,450 --> 00:10:28,933
were likely to learn things.

200
00:10:28,933 --> 00:10:30,850
But if they don't play
the game, they are not.

201
00:10:30,850 --> 00:10:33,930
So we wanted to think about
increasing student persistence

202
00:10:33,930 --> 00:10:36,270
in terms of the
number of levels.

203
00:10:36,270 --> 00:10:39,990
And so we really wanted to
go beyond expert performance

204
00:10:39,990 --> 00:10:42,570
in this case, like beyond
what the experts had done.

205
00:10:42,570 --> 00:10:45,393
And so what we did is we
used reinforcement learning,

206
00:10:45,393 --> 00:10:48,060
and we wanted to see if we could
outperform essentially behavior

207
00:10:48,060 --> 00:10:48,840
cloning.

208
00:10:48,840 --> 00:10:51,550
And to give a spoiler
of the types of ideas

209
00:10:51,550 --> 00:10:53,200
we're going to see
today, in this case,

210
00:10:53,200 --> 00:10:55,658
we found we could learn a policy
that increased persistence

211
00:10:55,658 --> 00:10:57,480
by about 30%.

212
00:10:57,480 --> 00:11:00,130
And so that suggests that
in some of these domains,

213
00:11:00,130 --> 00:11:04,620
there may be essentially
be enough data and evidence

214
00:11:04,620 --> 00:11:07,410
to find new decision
policies that

215
00:11:07,410 --> 00:11:11,100
are substantially better than
what is being currently done.

216
00:11:11,100 --> 00:11:13,600
And so that's what inspires
me in my lab a lot,

217
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:16,350
is to think about where can
we use natural variation

218
00:11:16,350 --> 00:11:18,210
in the decisions
that are being made

219
00:11:18,210 --> 00:11:21,120
or past experiments that
were run in order to find

220
00:11:21,120 --> 00:11:24,360
substantially better decision
policies than are currently

221
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:24,960
being used.

222
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:28,750
Yeah, not a super relevant
question to the subject matter,

223
00:11:28,750 --> 00:11:33,820
but just out of curiosity, was
the 30% distributed uniformly?

224
00:11:33,820 --> 00:11:36,780
Or was it just like the
people who already played

225
00:11:36,780 --> 00:11:39,630
played longer, or the
ones that stopped early

226
00:11:39,630 --> 00:11:40,870
would actually continue?

227
00:11:40,870 --> 00:11:42,040
This is a great question.

228
00:11:42,040 --> 00:11:43,950
So this is a really
big challenge often

229
00:11:43,950 --> 00:11:46,560
is whether or not who
are you moving inside

230
00:11:46,560 --> 00:11:47,440
of this distribution?

231
00:11:47,440 --> 00:11:49,148
So this is just an
expectation, like most

232
00:11:49,148 --> 00:11:51,300
of what we've been doing
for Q-learning and others.

233
00:11:51,300 --> 00:11:54,180
We did not analyze that
too much in this case.

234
00:11:54,180 --> 00:11:56,972
In another much more
recent paper we have,

235
00:11:56,972 --> 00:11:58,930
which I think came out
in January or something,

236
00:11:58,930 --> 00:12:01,350
we did exactly that
analysis to try to see

237
00:12:01,350 --> 00:12:02,645
who was actually impacted.

238
00:12:02,645 --> 00:12:04,020
And there, we were
really excited

239
00:12:04,020 --> 00:12:05,603
that it was the
lowest performers that

240
00:12:05,603 --> 00:12:06,930
were most impacted.

241
00:12:06,930 --> 00:12:09,210
And that was exciting because
one of the big concerns

242
00:12:09,210 --> 00:12:11,400
is that a lot of
these systems are just

243
00:12:11,400 --> 00:12:13,830
increasing the inequity gap.

244
00:12:13,830 --> 00:12:17,250
And this is particularly a
problem in these optional ones

245
00:12:17,250 --> 00:12:20,040
because it's normally the
kids that are furthest ahead

246
00:12:20,040 --> 00:12:22,530
that have the highest usage.

247
00:12:22,530 --> 00:12:23,680
So great question.

248
00:12:23,680 --> 00:12:26,200
But it also raises in terms
of on the technical side,

249
00:12:26,200 --> 00:12:28,365
these questions
around understanding--

250
00:12:28,365 --> 00:12:31,410
sort of predicting estimates
for subparts of the population

251
00:12:31,410 --> 00:12:33,510
and doing heterogeneous
treatment effect analysis

252
00:12:33,510 --> 00:12:35,520
to figure out which
groupings of contexts

253
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:38,100
have different
forms of Q values.

254
00:12:38,100 --> 00:12:39,390
Yeah.

255
00:12:39,390 --> 00:12:41,790
In terms of the
policy, what was sort

256
00:12:41,790 --> 00:12:44,340
of being changed, like
the simplest level,

257
00:12:44,340 --> 00:12:46,390
is it like the difficulty
of the fractions?

258
00:12:46,390 --> 00:12:49,050
Is it how hard it goes
as they're going up?

259
00:12:49,050 --> 00:12:50,260
Yeah, it's a great question.

260
00:12:50,260 --> 00:12:52,532
So in this case, I can't
remember what the final exact

261
00:12:52,532 --> 00:12:54,990
policy was using, but the type
of things that we're varying

262
00:12:54,990 --> 00:12:57,310
in this case is things
around the fraction--

263
00:12:57,310 --> 00:12:58,560
so like changing the numbers--

264
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:00,810
as well as different
things of how tricky

265
00:13:00,810 --> 00:13:03,332
it is graphically to do that.

266
00:13:03,332 --> 00:13:05,040
So there was a couple
of different things

267
00:13:05,040 --> 00:13:07,890
that we could
manipulate as well as--

268
00:13:07,890 --> 00:13:10,300
you can see, just
like visually here,

269
00:13:10,300 --> 00:13:11,500
these look quite different.

270
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:14,430
So one thing that we found
in some other work in a game

271
00:13:14,430 --> 00:13:16,350
called Battleship
Numberline, which

272
00:13:16,350 --> 00:13:19,230
I was excited because
recently, my son uses BrainPOP.

273
00:13:19,230 --> 00:13:20,200
And it just popped up.

274
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:21,630
And I was like,
we worked on that.

275
00:13:21,630 --> 00:13:23,250
So that was exciting.

276
00:13:23,250 --> 00:13:25,950
In Battleship Numberline,
which is another thing

277
00:13:25,950 --> 00:13:28,050
to do with fractions,
we found there

278
00:13:28,050 --> 00:13:30,970
that variability was incredibly
important for persistence.

279
00:13:30,970 --> 00:13:34,240
And so just changing how
things look in that case,

280
00:13:34,240 --> 00:13:37,530
how big the battleships were,
also makes a very big difference

281
00:13:37,530 --> 00:13:38,872
to persistence and engagement.

282
00:13:38,872 --> 00:13:40,830
I think that's actually
an interesting question

283
00:13:40,830 --> 00:13:44,490
too, in terms of
including the history

284
00:13:44,490 --> 00:13:48,000
and the state features to try
to capture stuff like people

285
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,730
caring about variability.

286
00:13:50,730 --> 00:13:52,000
So great questions.

287
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,780
We'll talk a little bit more
about this example later

288
00:13:54,780 --> 00:13:56,820
to talk about some
things that we tried

289
00:13:56,820 --> 00:13:59,280
or that didn't work
in this domain.

290
00:13:59,280 --> 00:14:01,530
But this is just to
highlight that, I guess,

291
00:14:01,530 --> 00:14:03,400
we shouldn't set our
expectations too low.

292
00:14:03,400 --> 00:14:05,980
So I think that imitation
learning is amazing.

293
00:14:05,980 --> 00:14:08,170
And of course, if
you're trying to imitate

294
00:14:08,170 --> 00:14:10,340
the best surgeons in the
world, that's incredible.

295
00:14:10,340 --> 00:14:11,715
But there are many
cases where we

296
00:14:11,715 --> 00:14:13,670
think we can go beyond
human performance,

297
00:14:13,670 --> 00:14:17,200
particularly in cases where
our high-level principles don't

298
00:14:17,200 --> 00:14:19,550
inform what we should do
at a more micro level.

299
00:14:19,550 --> 00:14:22,390
So, for example, here we
might have general principles

300
00:14:22,390 --> 00:14:23,360
of learning science.

301
00:14:23,360 --> 00:14:26,320
But it doesn't say which
activity to exactly do when,

302
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:30,680
and that's where it being data
driven can be really helpful.

303
00:14:30,680 --> 00:14:32,180
OK, let me give you
another example.

304
00:14:32,180 --> 00:14:34,055
Another place thing that
we think about a lot

305
00:14:34,055 --> 00:14:34,790
is health care.

306
00:14:34,790 --> 00:14:36,790
We've collaborated a lot
with Finale Doshi-Velez

307
00:14:36,790 --> 00:14:38,480
at Harvard and her lab.

308
00:14:38,480 --> 00:14:41,540
This is an example,
thinking about hypertension

309
00:14:41,540 --> 00:14:44,540
and trying to optimize
different policies for that.

310
00:14:44,540 --> 00:14:46,190
There is a really
amazing data set

311
00:14:46,190 --> 00:14:49,730
called Mimic that comes out
of, I think, MIT and MGH,

312
00:14:49,730 --> 00:14:52,220
Mass General Hospital,
which has lots and lots

313
00:14:52,220 --> 00:14:54,630
of electronic medical
record systems.

314
00:14:54,630 --> 00:14:58,700
And so what these guys did
in this particular paper is

315
00:14:58,700 --> 00:15:00,960
to look at behavior policy--

316
00:15:00,960 --> 00:15:02,250
so that's this flat line--

317
00:15:02,250 --> 00:15:04,310
and to see if they
could learn policies

318
00:15:04,310 --> 00:15:06,980
using a method called
popcorn that they thought

319
00:15:06,980 --> 00:15:08,100
would be much better.

320
00:15:08,100 --> 00:15:10,925
And again, here the results
depend on the method

321
00:15:10,925 --> 00:15:13,050
and some of the hyperparameters
they're looking at.

322
00:15:13,050 --> 00:15:14,685
But the important
thing just to notice

323
00:15:14,685 --> 00:15:16,310
here is that a number
of these policies

324
00:15:16,310 --> 00:15:19,770
are substantially better than
baseline, suggesting again,

325
00:15:19,770 --> 00:15:21,350
that there may be
domains where we

326
00:15:21,350 --> 00:15:23,600
can leverage the intrinsic
variability in the data

327
00:15:23,600 --> 00:15:26,180
and identify things that are
working much more successfully

328
00:15:26,180 --> 00:15:29,780
in this systematic way.

329
00:15:29,780 --> 00:15:31,290
So when we think
about doing this,

330
00:15:31,290 --> 00:15:33,740
generally, I would call this
sort of offline or batch

331
00:15:33,740 --> 00:15:34,770
or counterfactual.

332
00:15:34,770 --> 00:15:37,430
And it's counterfactual
because what we're trying to do

333
00:15:37,430 --> 00:15:39,860
is to estimate or
learn policies that

334
00:15:39,860 --> 00:15:42,950
don't exist in the actual
data collection strategy.

335
00:15:42,950 --> 00:15:45,740
So we have this setting
now where we'll assume,

336
00:15:45,740 --> 00:15:47,510
like in imitation
learning, that we have

337
00:15:47,510 --> 00:15:49,205
a data set of n trajectories.

338
00:15:49,205 --> 00:15:51,080
So we're going to assume
now we're going back

339
00:15:51,080 --> 00:15:52,920
to the standard MDP setting.

340
00:15:52,920 --> 00:15:54,690
And it's not
pairwise preferences.

341
00:15:54,690 --> 00:15:57,740
We're just back to having
sequences of states and actions

342
00:15:57,740 --> 00:15:59,210
and rewards.

343
00:15:59,210 --> 00:16:04,300
So all right, so in particular,
we may have things like this

344
00:16:04,300 --> 00:16:06,400
where we have data
from one policy

345
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:07,900
and data from another
policy, and we

346
00:16:07,900 --> 00:16:10,360
want to think about how we
can learn from that, thinking

347
00:16:10,360 --> 00:16:13,420
about the state distribution
of what's actually best.

348
00:16:13,420 --> 00:16:19,560
Now, I'll just highlight here
two reasons why this is hard.

349
00:16:19,560 --> 00:16:24,150
So we're always trying to
estimate a counterfactual here

350
00:16:24,150 --> 00:16:27,330
over what might have
happened that wasn't tried.

351
00:16:27,330 --> 00:16:30,133
So in this case, we don't know
for this patient group what

352
00:16:30,133 --> 00:16:32,550
would have happened if we gave
them that treatment or vice

353
00:16:32,550 --> 00:16:33,990
versa.

354
00:16:33,990 --> 00:16:36,570
So just a reminder, this
is the fundamental problem

355
00:16:36,570 --> 00:16:38,860
of causal inference.

356
00:16:38,860 --> 00:16:41,050
And this is going to be a
big challenge for us here,

357
00:16:41,050 --> 00:16:43,320
particularly when we try to
go beyond the performance

358
00:16:43,320 --> 00:16:46,410
of the policy we
saw in the past.

359
00:16:46,410 --> 00:16:48,745
So data is censored.

360
00:16:48,745 --> 00:16:50,370
And of course, in
general, again, we're

361
00:16:50,370 --> 00:16:51,995
going to need
generalization because we

362
00:16:51,995 --> 00:16:56,570
don't want to have to enumerate
all the possible policies.

363
00:16:56,570 --> 00:16:59,180
And I do just want
to highlight here

364
00:16:59,180 --> 00:17:01,400
that in addition to
education and health care,

365
00:17:01,400 --> 00:17:03,980
you want to think about climate
change or many other areas.

366
00:17:03,980 --> 00:17:06,037
There's just a huge
number of scenarios,

367
00:17:06,037 --> 00:17:08,329
including robotics, because
it's often really expensive

368
00:17:08,329 --> 00:17:11,089
to do robotics experiments
where these types of ideas

369
00:17:11,089 --> 00:17:13,280
are helpful.

370
00:17:13,280 --> 00:17:15,829
Now, one thing you
might be wondering about

371
00:17:15,829 --> 00:17:17,480
is, when I'm talking
about this and I'm

372
00:17:17,480 --> 00:17:18,980
talking about
trying to understand

373
00:17:18,980 --> 00:17:21,710
the performance of a new
decision policy that was not

374
00:17:21,710 --> 00:17:24,140
used to gather the
data, you might start

375
00:17:24,140 --> 00:17:25,730
to think back to Q-learning.

376
00:17:25,730 --> 00:17:28,069
There's a lot of work on
off-policy reinforcement

377
00:17:28,069 --> 00:17:30,920
learning from really the very
beginning of reinforcement

378
00:17:30,920 --> 00:17:31,660
learning.

379
00:17:31,660 --> 00:17:34,040
So you might say, why don't
we already have the tools

380
00:17:34,040 --> 00:17:37,340
that we need to try to tackle
this problem of learning better

381
00:17:37,340 --> 00:17:38,370
policies?

382
00:17:38,370 --> 00:17:42,810
And in fact, as we saw, ChatGPT,
if we learn a reward function

383
00:17:42,810 --> 00:17:45,840
and we do PPO, that is
doing off-policy learning.

384
00:17:45,840 --> 00:17:49,130
So that's one example.

385
00:17:49,130 --> 00:17:50,423
So why can't we do this?

386
00:17:50,423 --> 00:17:53,090
Why can we just do Q-learning or
some of the other methods we've

387
00:17:53,090 --> 00:17:54,830
seen?

388
00:17:54,830 --> 00:17:57,530
One thing to remember
is a little while ago,

389
00:17:57,530 --> 00:17:59,510
I said, sometimes we
have this deadly triad

390
00:17:59,510 --> 00:18:02,480
of bootstrapping function,
approximation and off-policy

391
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:03,940
learning.

392
00:18:03,940 --> 00:18:06,760
That sometimes when we combine
all three of these things.

393
00:18:06,760 --> 00:18:07,960
Things can fail.

394
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:10,270
And that was part of
the motivation for PPO,

395
00:18:10,270 --> 00:18:13,290
is that we don't want to go
too far from the distribution.

396
00:18:13,290 --> 00:18:15,270
Let me just talk a
little bit about what

397
00:18:15,270 --> 00:18:17,460
can happen here in the
context of Q-learning sort

398
00:18:17,460 --> 00:18:20,560
of model-free learning.

399
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:25,690
So this is the BCQ, Behavior
Constrained Q-learning,

400
00:18:25,690 --> 00:18:28,210
from Scott Fujimoto.

401
00:18:28,210 --> 00:18:31,092
And what this shows here
is that-- so these are

402
00:18:31,092 --> 00:18:32,300
a bunch of different methods.

403
00:18:32,300 --> 00:18:35,320
This is DQN, Deep Q-learning.

404
00:18:35,320 --> 00:18:37,600
This is behavior cloning.

405
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:39,920
This is the behavioral policy.

406
00:18:39,920 --> 00:18:42,187
So what they did is
they gathered some data,

407
00:18:42,187 --> 00:18:44,020
and then they tried to
use different methods

408
00:18:44,020 --> 00:18:45,850
to learn a policy from it.

409
00:18:45,850 --> 00:18:48,620
And this is DPG.

410
00:18:48,620 --> 00:18:51,070
And what they found with this
is that some of the methods

411
00:18:51,070 --> 00:18:53,830
did really bad, even
given the behavior data.

412
00:18:53,830 --> 00:18:57,410
DQN does about the same
as the behavior data.

413
00:18:57,410 --> 00:19:02,110
But what they found here is
that by being a bit more careful

414
00:19:02,110 --> 00:19:04,420
and using methods that
were explicitly designed

415
00:19:04,420 --> 00:19:06,470
to handle this offline data--

416
00:19:06,470 --> 00:19:09,010
in this case, BCQ
behavior, constrained

417
00:19:09,010 --> 00:19:12,620
Q-learning-- they could
do substantially better.

418
00:19:12,620 --> 00:19:14,570
And so that suggests
that we don't probably

419
00:19:14,570 --> 00:19:16,730
just want to use-- if we
know our data is fixed,

420
00:19:16,730 --> 00:19:18,938
and we know we're not going
to get additional data,

421
00:19:18,938 --> 00:19:20,480
that it may be worth
it for us to use

422
00:19:20,480 --> 00:19:22,490
different types of
algorithms in order

423
00:19:22,490 --> 00:19:24,470
to handle the fact that
our data is constrained

424
00:19:24,470 --> 00:19:27,980
and we're not going to be
continuing to get fresh data.

425
00:19:27,980 --> 00:19:31,910
So that motivates why we're
going to need new methods.

426
00:19:31,910 --> 00:19:32,410
All right.

427
00:19:32,410 --> 00:19:35,460
So now what we're going to do
is dive into policy evaluation,

428
00:19:35,460 --> 00:19:39,210
and then we'll talk about
policy optimization afterwards.

429
00:19:39,210 --> 00:19:43,210
So in batch policy
evaluation, what

430
00:19:43,210 --> 00:19:44,680
we're going to be
thinking about is

431
00:19:44,680 --> 00:19:47,120
we have a particular
policy of interest.

432
00:19:47,120 --> 00:19:48,738
And we have a data
set, and we'd like

433
00:19:48,738 --> 00:19:50,530
to be able to use that
data set to estimate

434
00:19:50,530 --> 00:19:53,110
how good that policy
is for one state

435
00:19:53,110 --> 00:19:57,010
or on average over a
set of starting states--

436
00:19:57,010 --> 00:20:01,550
so similar to what we've seen
for policy evaluation before.

437
00:20:01,550 --> 00:20:05,430
One thing I want to highlight--

438
00:20:05,430 --> 00:20:08,730
this is by Phil Thomas, who
I had the privilege of having

439
00:20:08,730 --> 00:20:10,120
as my postdoc a few years ago.

440
00:20:10,120 --> 00:20:13,680
He is a professor
at UMass Amherst.

441
00:20:13,680 --> 00:20:16,380
We generally want to think
about sample efficient methods

442
00:20:16,380 --> 00:20:17,410
for doing this.

443
00:20:17,410 --> 00:20:20,640
So in this case, he
was working with Adobe,

444
00:20:20,640 --> 00:20:23,830
and they have 10 million
to 20 million trajectories.

445
00:20:23,830 --> 00:20:26,770
It doesn't matter too
much what these lines are.

446
00:20:26,770 --> 00:20:30,330
The key thing here is that
this is the behavior policy,

447
00:20:30,330 --> 00:20:32,100
and you want to be
learning policies

448
00:20:32,100 --> 00:20:34,350
which you're confident are
better than your behavior

449
00:20:34,350 --> 00:20:35,252
policy.

450
00:20:35,252 --> 00:20:37,710
And this is just to highlight
that depending on the methods

451
00:20:37,710 --> 00:20:40,800
you use, you may be confident
at very different points,

452
00:20:40,800 --> 00:20:42,523
so just meaning
that data efficiency

453
00:20:42,523 --> 00:20:44,690
and having good algorithms
is going to matter a lot.

454
00:20:44,690 --> 00:20:45,190
Yeah.

455
00:20:45,190 --> 00:20:48,240
By behavior, you mean
the policy that was

456
00:20:48,240 --> 00:20:50,910
observed in the current set?

457
00:20:50,910 --> 00:20:52,360
Exactly-- behavior policy.

458
00:20:52,360 --> 00:20:53,860
Great, great
clarification question.

459
00:20:53,860 --> 00:20:55,735
When I say behavior
policy today, what I mean

460
00:20:55,735 --> 00:20:57,360
is the policy that
was used to gather

461
00:20:57,360 --> 00:20:58,870
the data set that you have.

462
00:20:58,870 --> 00:21:00,430
So I'll just write
that out on here.

463
00:21:03,030 --> 00:21:08,760
So we're going to assume
behavior policy is the one that

464
00:21:08,760 --> 00:21:10,130
was used to gather your data.

465
00:21:15,040 --> 00:21:15,540
All right.

466
00:21:15,540 --> 00:21:17,100
Let's first think
about using models.

467
00:21:17,100 --> 00:21:18,558
So this is actually
the first thing

468
00:21:18,558 --> 00:21:20,250
we tried to do with Refraction.

469
00:21:20,250 --> 00:21:22,190
We thought, OK, great.

470
00:21:22,190 --> 00:21:24,900
Travis Mendel, who is the grad
student leading the project,

471
00:21:24,900 --> 00:21:26,880
we have all this
historical data.

472
00:21:26,880 --> 00:21:29,090
Let's just try to
learn models from it.

473
00:21:29,090 --> 00:21:30,590
So we're going to
look at, and we're

474
00:21:30,590 --> 00:21:34,370
going to represent the
state space in some way.

475
00:21:34,370 --> 00:21:35,980
And then under
different actions--

476
00:21:35,980 --> 00:21:37,980
here in this case, that's
just different levels.

477
00:21:37,980 --> 00:21:40,397
And there's only a finite
number of levels and activities.

478
00:21:40,397 --> 00:21:42,980
Let's learn a dynamics model.

479
00:21:42,980 --> 00:21:46,310
So in this case, the idea is
that we have that existing

480
00:21:46,310 --> 00:21:48,900
data set, and we're going to
learn an explicit dynamics

481
00:21:48,900 --> 00:21:49,400
model.

482
00:21:49,400 --> 00:21:51,450
And we can learn an
explicit reward model.

483
00:21:51,450 --> 00:21:53,030
Now, in our case,
our reward model

484
00:21:53,030 --> 00:21:55,380
was known because
it's persistence.

485
00:21:55,380 --> 00:21:58,040
So we could essentially
get a reward every time

486
00:21:58,040 --> 00:21:59,930
the student didn't
quit the game,

487
00:21:59,930 --> 00:22:01,680
but we didn't know
the dynamics model.

488
00:22:01,680 --> 00:22:04,520
And so that's what we're
using the data to learn.

489
00:22:04,520 --> 00:22:07,070
Now, as you might imagine, we
had to make a lot of choices

490
00:22:07,070 --> 00:22:10,602
here about what state
representations you would use.

491
00:22:10,602 --> 00:22:13,310
And so we thought about lots and
lots and lots of different state

492
00:22:13,310 --> 00:22:15,950
representations.

493
00:22:15,950 --> 00:22:19,950
But once you have that, then you
can treat this as a simulator.

494
00:22:19,950 --> 00:22:21,953
So now you have your
simulator of the world

495
00:22:21,953 --> 00:22:24,120
because you have a dynamics
model or a reward model.

496
00:22:24,120 --> 00:22:26,053
Either you can do
this analytically,

497
00:22:26,053 --> 00:22:27,470
like in some of
the methods we saw

498
00:22:27,470 --> 00:22:29,750
in some of the
first few classes,

499
00:22:29,750 --> 00:22:33,910
or you can use dynamic
programming or Q-learning,

500
00:22:33,910 --> 00:22:37,970
Q-evaluation with those
to explicitly learn

501
00:22:37,970 --> 00:22:39,590
what the value is.

502
00:22:39,590 --> 00:22:40,940
But really, can use anything.

503
00:22:40,940 --> 00:22:42,750
You can even use like
Monte Carlo methods

