642
00:28:51,580 --> 00:28:53,430
We would like to
directly evaluate

643
00:28:53,430 --> 00:28:55,770
the performance of
a policy instead

644
00:28:55,770 --> 00:29:00,000
of using as a proxy, how much
our Q-function is changing

645
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:03,720
or how accurate we think
our dynamics model is.

646
00:29:03,720 --> 00:29:04,440
Yes.

647
00:29:04,440 --> 00:29:07,350
So when we evaluate
the policy, we

648
00:29:07,350 --> 00:29:10,800
execute it under
the real environment

649
00:29:10,800 --> 00:29:14,520
or estimate the policy
performance using our estimate.

650
00:29:14,520 --> 00:29:17,310
So there's two things.

651
00:29:17,310 --> 00:29:19,590
We can do it under
our simulated model,

652
00:29:19,590 --> 00:29:22,320
or we can do it
under our real model.

653
00:29:22,320 --> 00:29:24,510
We don't want to have to
do it under our real model

654
00:29:24,510 --> 00:29:27,030
because we want to know
which policy to deploy

655
00:29:27,030 --> 00:29:28,387
before we actually deploy it.

656
00:29:28,387 --> 00:29:30,220
Otherwise, we could
kind of be doing online.

657
00:29:30,220 --> 00:29:32,100
So what I'll shortly
be giving you

658
00:29:32,100 --> 00:29:35,190
is a way to get an accurate
estimate of how good the policy

659
00:29:35,190 --> 00:29:36,550
is before we deploy it.

660
00:29:36,550 --> 00:29:38,110
I haven't said how
to do that yet.

661
00:29:38,110 --> 00:29:40,892
I've just argued that using
models alone might not be good.

662
00:29:40,892 --> 00:29:41,850
Do you have a question?

663
00:29:41,850 --> 00:29:42,830
OK, yeah.

664
00:29:42,830 --> 00:29:47,110
So about model
misspecification is one way

665
00:29:47,110 --> 00:29:49,990
to think about this
just like you're

666
00:29:49,990 --> 00:29:53,230
kind of overfitting
your dynamics

667
00:29:53,230 --> 00:29:57,770
model by increasing the number
of states that you represented?

668
00:29:57,770 --> 00:29:59,290
It's a question.

669
00:29:59,290 --> 00:30:01,720
We're not overfitting
here because it really

670
00:30:01,720 --> 00:30:03,550
is a better fit.

671
00:30:03,550 --> 00:30:05,360
It's still just
not a perfect fit.

672
00:30:05,360 --> 00:30:08,000
In other ways, you might say
this is it's not realizable.

673
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:10,025
This is not the real
model of student learning.

674
00:30:10,025 --> 00:30:11,650
And under this, that
means that there's

675
00:30:11,650 --> 00:30:15,590
still essentially significant
bias when we do this learning.

676
00:30:19,680 --> 00:30:21,150
Now, one thing I
just want to note

677
00:30:21,150 --> 00:30:23,890
is model-based learning
can still be helpful.

678
00:30:23,890 --> 00:30:26,700
One thing that we may
want to do in this case

679
00:30:26,700 --> 00:30:28,680
is explicitly build
different models

680
00:30:28,680 --> 00:30:31,490
when we know we want to
evaluate different policies.

681
00:30:31,490 --> 00:30:33,370
So normally, when
we fit a model,

682
00:30:33,370 --> 00:30:36,600
we try to minimize the loss
under the data distribution

683
00:30:36,600 --> 00:30:38,190
of the behavior policy.

684
00:30:38,190 --> 00:30:41,020
So if you have a bunch of data
and you fit your dynamics model,

685
00:30:41,020 --> 00:30:43,440
you're essentially trying
to optimize for the accuracy

686
00:30:43,440 --> 00:30:45,160
over your behavior policy.

687
00:30:45,160 --> 00:30:47,430
But if you know that the
policy you want to evaluate

688
00:30:47,430 --> 00:30:50,100
is different, you
can actually change.

689
00:30:50,100 --> 00:30:52,570
So you can weigh your
errors separately.

690
00:30:52,570 --> 00:30:54,510
So this is a paper that
we did a few years ago

691
00:30:54,510 --> 00:30:58,320
with [INAUDIBLE]
and Omer Gottesman

692
00:30:58,320 --> 00:31:00,293
and others, which
just highlighted this,

693
00:31:00,293 --> 00:31:01,960
that you could change
your loss function

694
00:31:01,960 --> 00:31:04,727
and essentially up weigh
your accuracy over the state

695
00:31:04,727 --> 00:31:06,810
and action pairs that you
think you will encounter

696
00:31:06,810 --> 00:31:08,430
under a different policy.

697
00:31:08,430 --> 00:31:09,940
And that can help a lot.

698
00:31:09,940 --> 00:31:12,880
So you can see here this
was for a medical domain.

699
00:31:12,880 --> 00:31:17,290
And what you can see is that
this green here is ground truth.

700
00:31:17,290 --> 00:31:20,780
And what we found in this case--
so ours was our model here.

701
00:31:20,780 --> 00:31:23,787
And this is just if you fit
for the behavior policy.

702
00:31:23,787 --> 00:31:25,870
And what you can see is
by essentially reweighting

703
00:31:25,870 --> 00:31:28,720
your data, you can fit dynamics
models that are much better

704
00:31:28,720 --> 00:31:34,000
fit the type of dynamics
you'd see in the future.

705
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:37,143
But now I'm going to introduce
sort of model-free methods,

706
00:31:37,143 --> 00:31:39,310
and then we're going to get
into importance sampling

707
00:31:39,310 --> 00:31:42,640
as other ways to try to do this
policy evaluation that hopefully

708
00:31:42,640 --> 00:31:44,920
have different limitations
or less limitations

709
00:31:44,920 --> 00:31:46,395
compared to the
model-based method.

710
00:31:49,020 --> 00:31:51,960
So one of the first methods
that I'll talk about here

711
00:31:51,960 --> 00:31:53,940
is fitted Q evaluation.

712
00:31:53,940 --> 00:31:56,250
So fitted Q evaluation
is going to look pretty

713
00:31:56,250 --> 00:31:59,130
similar to deep Q-learning,
but there's just

714
00:31:59,130 --> 00:32:01,800
going to be a couple
important differences.

715
00:32:01,800 --> 00:32:04,860
So our data set here is a
bunch of just different tuples

716
00:32:04,860 --> 00:32:08,780
of state action
reward next state.

717
00:32:08,780 --> 00:32:13,070
Recall that our Q-function
Q pi is just going to be

718
00:32:13,070 --> 00:32:17,120
the immediate reward we got
from being in that si, a tuple--

719
00:32:17,120 --> 00:32:18,830
so whatever we saw
in our data set--

720
00:32:18,830 --> 00:32:25,050
and then we'll put in plus
gamma times V pi of S I plus 1.

721
00:32:25,050 --> 00:32:27,180
And then what we do
is we try to minimize

722
00:32:27,180 --> 00:32:30,810
the difference between this
under a parameterized function,

723
00:32:30,810 --> 00:32:34,920
just like what we saw with deep
Q-learning versus the observed

724
00:32:34,920 --> 00:32:35,505
data tuples.

725
00:32:35,505 --> 00:32:37,130
So you can think of
this as our target.

726
00:32:41,380 --> 00:32:44,150
And this is called
fitted Q evaluation.

727
00:32:44,150 --> 00:32:46,370
It's closely related to
something called FQI,

728
00:32:46,370 --> 00:32:58,630
which is fitted Q iteration,
which I think was around 2015,

729
00:32:58,630 --> 00:33:03,110
2005ish.

730
00:33:03,110 --> 00:33:05,420
And so this is very
similar to what we've seen

731
00:33:05,420 --> 00:33:07,610
with deep Q-learning before.

732
00:33:07,610 --> 00:33:08,940
We just fit this function.

733
00:33:08,940 --> 00:33:11,000
The key thing here is
that we want it to be

734
00:33:11,000 --> 00:33:13,750
for just a single policy pi.

735
00:33:13,750 --> 00:33:16,640
So we're not doing an argmax.

736
00:33:16,640 --> 00:33:21,380
So this is how the algorithm
works for fitted Q evaluation.

737
00:33:21,380 --> 00:33:23,450
We sort of initialize
our Q function randomly.

738
00:33:23,450 --> 00:33:25,490
It could be a deep Q Network.

739
00:33:25,490 --> 00:33:26,780
It could be something else.

740
00:33:26,780 --> 00:33:31,630
We compute the targets where
when we put in the next Q,

741
00:33:31,630 --> 00:33:37,150
we have to use the policy
we're interested in evaluating.

742
00:33:37,150 --> 00:33:39,880
So we're only doing this for
the actions we would take

743
00:33:39,880 --> 00:33:41,780
under the policy we care about.

744
00:33:41,780 --> 00:33:46,990
We build our training set of
Xs, actions, and our output Q

745
00:33:46,990 --> 00:33:48,975
target, and then we
fit our Q function.

746
00:33:52,790 --> 00:33:55,460
And so, again, the key
difference here compared to DQN

747
00:33:55,460 --> 00:33:58,700
is there's no max.

748
00:33:58,700 --> 00:34:10,474
We are fixing this part
only for a fixed pi.

749
00:34:13,830 --> 00:34:16,639
But aside from that, it
should look really similar.

750
00:34:16,639 --> 00:34:20,360
And so one of the--
so this was something

751
00:34:20,360 --> 00:34:22,880
that was very closely
related to a common algorithm

752
00:34:22,880 --> 00:34:24,380
for doing off-policy
learning, which

753
00:34:24,380 --> 00:34:27,860
is fitted Q iteration, excuse
me, which is very related

754
00:34:27,860 --> 00:34:29,688
to deep Q learning.

755
00:34:29,688 --> 00:34:31,730
And one of the things
people wanted to understand

756
00:34:31,730 --> 00:34:35,179
is whether this thing that was
working in practice actually

757
00:34:35,179 --> 00:34:37,409
had some theoretical
grounding behind it.

758
00:34:37,409 --> 00:34:38,840
Like, could we say
anything formal

759
00:34:38,840 --> 00:34:40,420
about how good
this approach was.

760
00:34:43,120 --> 00:34:45,032
So just to give
you an illustration

761
00:34:45,032 --> 00:34:46,449
of the types of
guarantees that we

762
00:34:46,449 --> 00:34:48,940
can get in this
case, what we want

763
00:34:48,940 --> 00:34:53,980
to look at in this situation
is to think about what

764
00:34:53,980 --> 00:34:56,090
is the generalization error.

765
00:34:56,090 --> 00:34:58,940
OK, let me put this in here.

766
00:34:58,940 --> 00:35:01,545
So I won't go through
the whole paper.

767
00:35:01,545 --> 00:35:03,170
I just want to give
you an illustration

768
00:35:03,170 --> 00:35:06,060
of the types of guarantees that
you might get in this setting.

769
00:35:06,060 --> 00:35:08,000
What they would like
to know in this case

770
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:10,880
is to compare the
difference between the value

771
00:35:10,880 --> 00:35:13,940
that you will compute under this
procedure versus the true value

772
00:35:13,940 --> 00:35:15,710
of the policy.

773
00:35:15,710 --> 00:35:17,940
This is your normal
discount factor.

774
00:35:17,940 --> 00:35:20,550
And then there's a whole bunch
of things that are additional.

775
00:35:20,550 --> 00:35:23,010
Let me highlight some
important things here.

776
00:35:23,010 --> 00:35:25,100
And here is the number
of samples you need.

777
00:35:25,100 --> 00:35:27,170
So n tells you
about how much data

778
00:35:27,170 --> 00:35:29,040
you're going to need
in order to do this.

779
00:35:29,040 --> 00:35:30,220
So this is how much data--

780
00:35:33,170 --> 00:35:36,110
much data.

781
00:35:36,110 --> 00:35:44,376
OK, epsilon is sort of your
desired target accuracy.

782
00:35:47,110 --> 00:35:50,750
This is one of the
really important things.

783
00:35:50,750 --> 00:35:53,500
So we're going to
have something called

784
00:35:53,500 --> 00:35:58,136
a concentratability coefficient.

785
00:36:02,870 --> 00:36:04,460
Concentratability
coefficient is going

786
00:36:04,460 --> 00:36:07,273
to be the difference essentially
between the distribution

787
00:36:07,273 --> 00:36:09,440
of state action pairs that
you have in your data set

788
00:36:09,440 --> 00:36:10,940
and the distribution
of state action

789
00:36:10,940 --> 00:36:13,250
pairs you would get under
your desired policy.

790
00:36:13,250 --> 00:36:15,860
So we saw this before
with PPO of thinking

791
00:36:15,860 --> 00:36:19,910
about these divergence in the
state action distributions--

792
00:36:19,910 --> 00:36:23,910
state action distributions.

793
00:36:23,910 --> 00:36:29,860
And it's also related to what
we'll call overlap later.

794
00:36:29,860 --> 00:36:32,195
So I won't go through all
the details in this case,

795
00:36:32,195 --> 00:36:34,570
but I want to just give an
illustration that people often

796
00:36:34,570 --> 00:36:37,450
think about trying to
understand if you have a data

797
00:36:37,450 --> 00:36:41,230
set of some behavior data,
how accurate you can hope

798
00:36:41,230 --> 00:36:43,750
to be of evaluating the
performance of the policy

799
00:36:43,750 --> 00:36:46,960
depends on your discount
factor, because that says

800
00:36:46,960 --> 00:36:49,120
how accurate you want to
be and how much you care

801
00:36:49,120 --> 00:36:51,430
about long-term
rewards, how much data

802
00:36:51,430 --> 00:36:55,270
you have in terms of your target
error, and how closely related

803
00:36:55,270 --> 00:36:56,800
your state action
distributions are

804
00:36:56,800 --> 00:37:01,460
from your training set to your
test set or your desired policy.

805
00:37:01,460 --> 00:37:05,500
Now one of the challenges
about this approach

806
00:37:05,500 --> 00:37:08,492
is that it generally still
relies on the Markov assumption.

807
00:37:08,492 --> 00:37:10,450
So we're still assuming
our data is all Markov,

808
00:37:10,450 --> 00:37:12,140
and it relies on our models.

809
00:37:12,140 --> 00:37:15,820
In these case, the sort of Q
functions being well specified.

810
00:37:15,820 --> 00:37:17,030
So what do I mean by that?

811
00:37:17,030 --> 00:37:19,600
It means that we really
can fit the Q function.

812
00:37:19,600 --> 00:37:22,660
There's some existing Q function
in the world for our policy,

813
00:37:22,660 --> 00:37:24,310
and we can really fit it.

814
00:37:24,310 --> 00:37:29,370
And if you say, for example--

815
00:37:29,370 --> 00:37:31,330
let's say that this
is your state space.

816
00:37:31,330 --> 00:37:35,090
It's just one-dimensional.

817
00:37:35,090 --> 00:37:37,880
And this is what your
true function looks like.

818
00:37:37,880 --> 00:37:40,920
You can imagine that it
looks something like this.

819
00:37:40,920 --> 00:37:42,800
And let's say that
you are restricting

820
00:37:42,800 --> 00:37:48,500
yourself to fit a line like
that with just two parameters.

821
00:37:48,500 --> 00:37:50,940
So in that case, even if you
had infinite amounts of data,

822
00:37:50,940 --> 00:37:52,230
you're still going to
have a lot of error.

823
00:37:52,230 --> 00:37:54,350
You're not going to be
able to fit the Q function.

824
00:37:54,350 --> 00:37:57,037
So these methods assume
typically realizability,

825
00:37:57,037 --> 00:37:58,620
meaning that if you
had infinite data,

826
00:37:58,620 --> 00:37:59,480
you could fit the function.

827
00:37:59,480 --> 00:38:01,522
The problem is that you
don't have infinite data.

828
00:38:01,522 --> 00:38:02,910
OK, all right.

829
00:38:02,910 --> 00:38:05,360
So now we're going to see a
really beautiful method called

830
00:38:05,360 --> 00:38:07,740
importance sampling, which
allows us to deal with this.

831
00:38:07,740 --> 00:38:10,410
We've seen sort of brief
ideas about this before,

832
00:38:10,410 --> 00:38:13,100
but I'm curious if anybody who's
seen this in other classes.

833
00:38:13,100 --> 00:38:15,890
Who's seen importance
sampling before?

834
00:38:15,890 --> 00:38:18,080
So just a couple of people.

835
00:38:18,080 --> 00:38:21,110
This is one of the
favorite ideas in CS234

836
00:38:21,110 --> 00:38:23,690
according to some past people.

837
00:38:23,690 --> 00:38:24,210
All right.

838
00:38:24,210 --> 00:38:25,980
So the idea what
is the motivation?

839
00:38:25,980 --> 00:38:28,670
So importance sampling is
an idea from statistics

840
00:38:28,670 --> 00:38:31,850
that we have imported over
into reinforcement learning.

841
00:38:31,850 --> 00:38:33,240
Why would we like to do this?

842
00:38:33,240 --> 00:38:35,740
Well, we want a method that
doesn't rely on the models being

843
00:38:35,740 --> 00:38:38,330
correct, meaning that we
can actually fit things

844
00:38:38,330 --> 00:38:41,318
with a two-layer deep
neural network or stuff,

845
00:38:41,318 --> 00:38:43,610
and that we don't have to
rely on the Markov assumption

846
00:38:43,610 --> 00:38:45,740
in the state space we're using.

847
00:38:45,740 --> 00:38:47,870
We saw before that we
could use Monte Carlo

848
00:38:47,870 --> 00:38:51,350
methods to accomplish this
for online policy evaluation.

849
00:38:51,350 --> 00:38:53,198
And now we want to do
this for offline data,

850
00:38:53,198 --> 00:38:55,490
meaning that we have data
from a different distribution

851
00:38:55,490 --> 00:38:57,650
from the policy we
want to evaluate.

852
00:38:57,650 --> 00:38:59,880
And the key challenge,
as has often been,

853
00:38:59,880 --> 00:39:03,110
is data distribution mismatch.

854
00:39:03,110 --> 00:39:05,880
OK, so here's how
importance sampling works.

855
00:39:05,880 --> 00:39:07,760
Let me just specify
what this means.

856
00:39:07,760 --> 00:39:11,360
Let's say we want to try to
understand the expected reward

857
00:39:11,360 --> 00:39:13,053
over a distribution of states.

858
00:39:13,053 --> 00:39:15,595
So for this part, you can just
think of x is equal to states.

859
00:39:19,480 --> 00:39:23,300
And R of x is equal to
the reward of a state.

860
00:39:23,300 --> 00:39:26,340
This works for very, very
general distributions.

861
00:39:26,340 --> 00:39:30,800
But you can think of that here
as just being a [INAUDIBLE].

862
00:39:30,800 --> 00:39:31,310
All right.

863
00:39:31,310 --> 00:39:32,610
What we're going to
do is the following.

864
00:39:32,610 --> 00:39:34,360
This is what we would
like to evaluate.

865
00:39:34,360 --> 00:39:36,380
So you could think
of this here as maybe

866
00:39:36,380 --> 00:39:43,940
being p of x could be equal
to the probability of reaching

867
00:39:43,940 --> 00:39:50,330
x under policy.

868
00:39:50,330 --> 00:39:51,580
So you might really want this.

869
00:39:51,580 --> 00:39:53,830
You might want to know what
is the expected reward I'm

870
00:39:53,830 --> 00:39:56,910
going to get under this policy
where I know what my reward is

871
00:39:56,910 --> 00:39:58,630
for each state or I
have samples of it,

872
00:39:58,630 --> 00:40:00,760
and then I have this
probability distribution.

873
00:40:00,760 --> 00:40:03,040
The problem is that you
don't have data from that.

874
00:40:03,040 --> 00:40:09,020
So we put no data from p of x.

875
00:40:09,020 --> 00:40:11,290
So that's the general
challenge we're in.

876
00:40:11,290 --> 00:40:14,060
We want to see how well our
alternative policy would

877
00:40:14,060 --> 00:40:15,960
work for helping
students persist,

878
00:40:15,960 --> 00:40:17,840
but we have no data from that.

879
00:40:17,840 --> 00:40:20,630
So here's the trick.

880
00:40:20,630 --> 00:40:24,380
Let's multiply and
divide by the same thing.

881
00:40:24,380 --> 00:40:31,530
I'm going to introduce a new
policy and its distribution Q

882
00:40:31,530 --> 00:40:32,140
OK.

883
00:40:32,140 --> 00:40:35,615
So q of x is a different policy.

884
00:40:42,065 --> 00:40:43,190
This is a different policy.

885
00:40:43,190 --> 00:40:44,470
Maybe it's going to end
up in different states

886
00:40:44,470 --> 00:40:46,040
with different probabilities.

887
00:40:46,040 --> 00:40:48,810
OK, so let's rewrite this.

888
00:40:48,810 --> 00:40:53,840
This is going to be
equal to q of x times p

889
00:40:53,840 --> 00:41:00,620
of x over r over q of x r of x.

890
00:41:00,620 --> 00:41:03,330
OK, I haven't
changed anything yet.

891
00:41:03,330 --> 00:41:04,850
This is exactly equal.

892
00:41:04,850 --> 00:41:07,100
But if I have data
from q of x, I

893
00:41:07,100 --> 00:41:10,610
can approximate this
excitation with samples.

894
00:41:10,610 --> 00:41:14,630
So this is approximately
equal to 1 over n sum

895
00:41:14,630 --> 00:41:19,850
over I equals 1
to n of x sampled

896
00:41:19,850 --> 00:41:28,820
according to q of x, p
of xi, q of xi, r of xi.

897
00:41:31,410 --> 00:41:32,750
This is super beautiful.

898
00:41:32,750 --> 00:41:34,950
What we've said here
is that I really

899
00:41:34,950 --> 00:41:38,480
want to estimate the expectation
of something over, say, policy,

900
00:41:38,480 --> 00:41:39,750
this policy p.

901
00:41:39,750 --> 00:41:41,370
I don't have any samples from p.

902
00:41:41,370 --> 00:41:45,450
What I can do is I can just
take samples from my policy q,

903
00:41:45,450 --> 00:41:48,480
and I can reweigh them.

904
00:41:48,480 --> 00:41:52,440
So it says, if I was
really likely to take--

905
00:41:52,440 --> 00:41:54,900
to reach a particular
x under a policy q

906
00:41:54,900 --> 00:41:57,960
but less likely under this
one, I'll weigh that data less.

907
00:41:57,960 --> 00:42:00,510
If I'm much more likely
to get to a state xi

908
00:42:00,510 --> 00:42:03,200
than I was under here, I'm
going to upweight those samples.

909
00:42:05,760 --> 00:42:08,140
So this is beautiful,
and it's unbiased.

910
00:42:08,140 --> 00:42:12,900
So this is an unbiased estimate.

911
00:42:12,900 --> 00:42:16,050
We'll extend it in a second to
think about multi time steps,

912
00:42:16,050 --> 00:42:18,090
but just for single
time step right now,

913
00:42:18,090 --> 00:42:19,520
this is how we can do this.

914
00:42:19,520 --> 00:42:21,360
It gives us an
unbiased estimate.

915
00:42:21,360 --> 00:42:24,120
And as we'll see shortly, we can
extend this to multi time steps.

916
00:42:24,120 --> 00:42:27,090
And we don't have to
make a Markov assumption.

917
00:42:27,090 --> 00:42:30,880
OK, so this is a
really lovely idea.

918
00:42:30,880 --> 00:42:33,100
So we can compute
this expected value

919
00:42:33,100 --> 00:42:34,726
under an alternative
distribution.

920
00:42:37,910 --> 00:42:40,640
And it is generally
an unbiased estimator

921
00:42:40,640 --> 00:42:42,360
under a couple of assumptions.

922
00:42:42,360 --> 00:42:45,020
The first is that the
sampling distribution Q--

923
00:42:45,020 --> 00:42:46,970
so our alternative policy--

924
00:42:46,970 --> 00:42:51,560
has to be greater than or equal
to 0 for all x such that P of x

925
00:42:51,560 --> 00:42:53,120
would be greater than zero.

926
00:42:53,120 --> 00:42:54,880
What does that mean in practice?

927
00:42:54,880 --> 00:42:58,490
That means that if you could
reach a state under your policy

928
00:42:58,490 --> 00:43:01,055
you care about with a
non-zero probability.

929
00:43:01,055 --> 00:43:02,180
So let's say, I don't know.

930
00:43:02,180 --> 00:43:04,220
Your student could get
to this particular level

931
00:43:04,220 --> 00:43:07,620
with non-zero probability
under your target policy,

932
00:43:07,620 --> 00:43:10,730
then there has to be some
probability you'd also get there

933
00:43:10,730 --> 00:43:14,333
under your training data set.

934
00:43:14,333 --> 00:43:15,750
This is sort of
reasonable, right.

935
00:43:15,750 --> 00:43:19,320
So this says that if I
want to think about--

936
00:43:19,320 --> 00:43:23,960
I don't know-- a policy that
like recommends restaurants

937
00:43:23,960 --> 00:43:28,070
versus coffee shops, I can't
use that data to then estimate

938
00:43:28,070 --> 00:43:30,060
how good it would be
to go to the movies,

939
00:43:30,060 --> 00:43:32,360
because I've never done that.

940
00:43:32,360 --> 00:43:34,380
For anything that we're
trying to estimate here,

941
00:43:34,380 --> 00:43:38,110
we have to have non-zero
probability for that x.

942
00:43:38,110 --> 00:43:40,100
The second thing is a
little bit more subtle,

943
00:43:40,100 --> 00:43:42,520
but it comes up a lot in
real empirical data, which is

944
00:43:42,520 --> 00:43:44,240
called no hidden confounding.

945
00:43:44,240 --> 00:43:45,790
And that means that
essentially you

946
00:43:45,790 --> 00:43:48,820
have to know all of the
features that were used

947
00:43:48,820 --> 00:43:52,790
to define this distribution.

948
00:43:52,790 --> 00:43:55,228
So this doesn't-- may not
seem as clear in this part,

949
00:43:55,228 --> 00:43:57,520
but I think once we start
getting into multi time steps

950
00:43:57,520 --> 00:43:59,960
and the sequences, it
becomes really relevant.

951
00:43:59,960 --> 00:44:01,050
So let me give an example.

952
00:44:34,250 --> 00:44:36,187
OK, so imagine like a
health care setting.

953
00:44:36,187 --> 00:44:38,270
So if we go back to that
electronic medical record

954
00:44:38,270 --> 00:44:40,760
setting, we often are
interested in what

955
00:44:40,760 --> 00:44:43,350
would have happened to a patient
if we did a different action.

956
00:44:43,350 --> 00:44:45,920
So we want to know what
that counterfactual is.

957
00:44:45,920 --> 00:44:48,110
One of the challenges
there is that we

958
00:44:48,110 --> 00:44:49,860
will have certain
features that are

959
00:44:49,860 --> 00:44:52,680
in our electronic
medical record system.

960
00:44:52,680 --> 00:44:55,520
We will see an action, like
someone was taken to surgery

961
00:44:55,520 --> 00:44:58,760
or some drug was administered,
and then we see the outcome.

962
00:44:58,760 --> 00:45:01,340
In order for importance
sampling to work,

963
00:45:01,340 --> 00:45:04,400
all of the features that were
used to make that decision

964
00:45:04,400 --> 00:45:08,480
or pick that action
have to be known.

965
00:45:08,480 --> 00:45:11,030
And that's called no
hidden confounding.

966
00:45:11,030 --> 00:45:12,090
Now, why is that?

967
00:45:12,090 --> 00:45:14,030
Well, it might be,
for example, you

968
00:45:14,030 --> 00:45:17,870
might see that there are
certain patients that are sick,

969
00:45:17,870 --> 00:45:20,010
and then a particular
action is taken.

970
00:45:20,010 --> 00:45:20,802
And maybe they die.

971
00:45:20,802 --> 00:45:22,343
And you might see
other patients that

972
00:45:22,343 --> 00:45:24,110
look like they have
the same features,

973
00:45:24,110 --> 00:45:25,590
and a different action is taken.

974
00:45:25,590 --> 00:45:26,630
And they live.

975
00:45:26,630 --> 00:45:30,170
And in that case, you might
think, oh, maybe the decision

976
00:45:30,170 --> 00:45:31,330
was just bad.

977
00:45:31,330 --> 00:45:32,330
That's possible.

978
00:45:32,330 --> 00:45:34,080
But it's also possible
that there are just

979
00:45:34,080 --> 00:45:36,760
hidden additional features that
you don't have in your data.

980
00:45:36,760 --> 00:45:39,300
And that meant that the first
person was much more sick,

981
00:45:39,300 --> 00:45:41,370
and that's why they got
that particular treatment

982
00:45:41,370 --> 00:45:43,590
versus the other person.

983
00:45:43,590 --> 00:45:48,480
So it might be that there's
important reasons that are not

984
00:45:48,480 --> 00:45:51,360
part of x that are being
used and used to define what

985
00:45:51,360 --> 00:45:54,010
the action is in the data set.

986
00:45:54,010 --> 00:45:54,510
Excuse me.

987
00:45:54,510 --> 00:45:56,600
And in those sorts of
confounding scenarios,

988
00:45:56,600 --> 00:45:58,350
and if you try to use
importance sampling,

989
00:45:58,350 --> 00:46:01,100
you will not get an
unbiased estimator.

990
00:46:01,100 --> 00:46:03,810
This is really important
and really hard in practice.

991
00:46:03,810 --> 00:46:04,980
It comes up all the time.

992
00:46:04,980 --> 00:46:06,772
And in fact, one of
the things we were just

993
00:46:06,772 --> 00:46:08,930
doing on a paper
we just put online,

994
00:46:08,930 --> 00:46:11,180
we were trying to think
really, really carefully about

995
00:46:11,180 --> 00:46:13,880
whether or not there would
be additional confounding

996
00:46:13,880 --> 00:46:17,510
beyond the features that
we had in our data set.

997
00:46:17,510 --> 00:46:19,280
So in that case, we
had done an experiment

998
