01:03:08,360 --> 01:03:10,878
is just your policy probability.

1245
01:03:10,878 --> 01:03:13,170
It just says, what action
would you take in this state.

1246
01:03:13,170 --> 01:03:16,530
And so this is known if you're
doing policy evaluation.

1247
01:03:16,530 --> 01:03:19,050
So this first introduced
for RL, to my knowledge

1248
01:03:19,050 --> 01:03:23,577
by Doina Precup, Richard Sutton,
and Satinder Singh in 2000.

1249
01:03:23,577 --> 01:03:25,910
Then there's been a lot of
follow-up work and leveraging

1250
01:03:25,910 --> 01:03:27,560
of this.

1251
01:03:27,560 --> 01:03:28,580
It's super helpful.

1252
01:03:28,580 --> 01:03:31,920
We don't need the Markov
assumption or anything.

1253
01:03:31,920 --> 01:03:34,590
OK, requires
fundamental assumptions.

1254
01:03:34,590 --> 01:03:38,300
It's unbiased, and it corrects
for distribution mismatch--

1255
01:03:38,300 --> 01:03:40,090
so extremely helpful.

1256
01:03:40,090 --> 01:03:42,590
I won't do this now, but you
might want to look through this

1257
01:03:42,590 --> 01:03:46,220
later just to think about, given
everything you know about Monte

1258
01:03:46,220 --> 01:03:48,230
Carlo methods, et
cetera, like what

1259
01:03:48,230 --> 01:03:52,090
might be some of the
limitations of doing this?

1260
01:03:52,090 --> 01:03:53,620
I'll just briefly
say there's been

1261
01:03:53,620 --> 01:03:55,040
a whole bunch of extensions.

1262
01:03:55,040 --> 01:03:57,610
One thing is called per
decision importance sampling.

1263
01:03:57,610 --> 01:04:00,910
Similar to a policy gradient,
we think about the fact

1264
01:04:00,910 --> 01:04:04,810
that in terms of the
reward and the decisions,

1265
01:04:04,810 --> 01:04:08,270
later decisions that are made
can't affect earlier rewards.

1266
01:04:08,270 --> 01:04:10,090
So you can reduce
the variance by being

1267
01:04:10,090 --> 01:04:14,120
a little bit more strategic
in where you put your weights.

1268
01:04:14,120 --> 01:04:17,290
And we saw similar ideas
to this in policy gradient.

1269
01:04:17,290 --> 01:04:19,760
So this is called the per
decision importance sampling,

1270
01:04:19,760 --> 01:04:23,860
and it helps to have better
properties in terms of--

1271
01:04:23,860 --> 01:04:27,700
particularly for long sequences.

1272
01:04:27,700 --> 01:04:29,600
In general, the
variance is pretty high,

1273
01:04:29,600 --> 01:04:31,230
like for most Monte
Carlo methods.

1274
01:04:33,990 --> 01:04:36,745
One thing to know is there's
concentration inequalities,

1275
01:04:36,745 --> 01:04:38,370
like the Hoeffding
inequality, that you

1276
01:04:38,370 --> 01:04:40,820
can use that will generally
scale with the largest

1277
01:04:40,820 --> 01:04:43,320
range of the variable if you
want to start to get confidence

1278
01:04:43,320 --> 01:04:45,940
intervals over these values.

1279
01:04:45,940 --> 01:04:49,350
And this can start to be pretty
terrible for long horizons

1280
01:04:49,350 --> 01:04:50,710
for importance sampling.

1281
01:04:50,710 --> 01:04:52,837
So I'll post afterwards
what the solutions are

1282
01:04:52,837 --> 01:04:54,670
for both of these check
your understandings,

1283
01:04:54,670 --> 01:04:57,270
but it's pretty informative
to think about exactly how

1284
01:04:57,270 --> 01:04:59,510
bad this can become.

1285
01:04:59,510 --> 01:05:02,930
OK to deal with this, there's
a lot of different extensions.

1286
01:05:02,930 --> 01:05:06,270
One thing is that you can if
you do have Markov structure,

1287
01:05:06,270 --> 01:05:09,290
you can think about state
distributions instead

1288
01:05:09,290 --> 01:05:11,550
of trajectories, and
that can be very helpful.

1289
01:05:11,550 --> 01:05:13,850
There's been a bunch of
work in that direction.

1290
01:05:13,850 --> 01:05:17,000
One work that we've done
in others is called--

1291
01:05:17,000 --> 01:05:20,120
is taking ideas from statistics
on doubly robust estimation

1292
01:05:20,120 --> 01:05:24,420
and using these to reduce the
variance in these methods,

1293
01:05:24,420 --> 01:05:26,900
as well as trying to blend
between methods that make

1294
01:05:26,900 --> 01:05:31,250
a Markov assumption
and methods that don't.

1295
01:05:31,250 --> 01:05:33,400
All right.

1296
01:05:33,400 --> 01:05:36,040
I want to finish now by
talking a bit about how

1297
01:05:36,040 --> 01:05:37,810
we can use these ideas
and others to think

1298
01:05:37,810 --> 01:05:39,852
about offline policy learning.

1299
01:05:39,852 --> 01:05:42,310
So I think there's a couple
important ideas we went through

1300
01:05:42,310 --> 01:05:43,260
so far today.

1301
01:05:43,260 --> 01:05:46,300
One is that you can just build a
simulator from historical data,

1302
01:05:46,300 --> 01:05:48,440
and you can use that to learn.

1303
01:05:48,440 --> 01:05:51,220
But it may be
biased, and that bias

1304
01:05:51,220 --> 01:05:53,200
may be substantial
when you're trying

1305
01:05:53,200 --> 01:05:55,360
to use it to pick policies.

1306
01:05:55,360 --> 01:05:58,403
We can do model-free
methods, but we're

1307
01:05:58,403 --> 01:06:00,070
going to want to be
careful about those.

1308
01:06:00,070 --> 01:06:01,460
And we're going to see
more of that later.

1309
01:06:01,460 --> 01:06:03,040
And you can use
importance sampling

1310
01:06:03,040 --> 01:06:05,925
to get an unbiased estimate,
but it might be high variance.

1311
01:06:05,925 --> 01:06:08,050
We're now going to think
about those sorts of ideas

1312
01:06:08,050 --> 01:06:10,900
in the context of when we're
actually trying to pick a policy

1313
01:06:10,900 --> 01:06:13,260
and do optimization.

1314
01:06:13,260 --> 01:06:15,577
So I'm going to go back
to this issue of coverage

1315
01:06:15,577 --> 01:06:17,410
because I think it's
important to emphasize.

1316
01:06:17,410 --> 01:06:20,215
So let's imagine that
you have antibiotics,

1317
01:06:20,215 --> 01:06:22,590
mechanical ventilator, and a
vasopressor, all things that

1318
01:06:22,590 --> 01:06:26,810
are often used in an
intensive care unit.

1319
01:06:26,810 --> 01:06:28,560
And you might have
different probabilities

1320
01:06:28,560 --> 01:06:30,010
of these interventions.

1321
01:06:30,010 --> 01:06:32,130
And let's say you
want to evaluate

1322
01:06:32,130 --> 01:06:36,620
a policy that frequently
does mechanical ventilation.

1323
01:06:36,620 --> 01:06:39,500
As we've been talking
about, your data

1324
01:06:39,500 --> 01:06:42,510
has to support the policy
you want to evaluate.

1325
01:06:42,510 --> 01:06:45,157
So if this is your
behavior policy, that

1326
01:06:45,157 --> 01:06:47,240
works because every single
action you want to try,

1327
01:06:47,240 --> 01:06:52,070
you have a non-zero probability
of drawing that in the data.

1328
01:06:52,070 --> 01:06:54,667
If you have this policy
that doesn't work,

1329
01:06:54,667 --> 01:06:56,750
that's the same as the
example that we saw before.

1330
01:06:56,750 --> 01:06:58,910
So if you never use a
vasopressor in your behavior

1331
01:06:58,910 --> 01:07:01,070
data, you cannot evaluate
how good that would be

1332
01:07:01,070 --> 01:07:02,840
in the future.

1333
01:07:02,840 --> 01:07:04,850
Now, when I draw like
this, or in the example

1334
01:07:04,850 --> 01:07:06,720
that I gave in the check
your understanding,

1335
01:07:06,720 --> 01:07:08,178
it's pretty obvious
because there's

1336
01:07:08,178 --> 01:07:10,800
a finite number of actions,
and it's pretty clear,

1337
01:07:10,800 --> 01:07:13,910
if we didn't take action, the
vasopressor action, that we

1338
01:07:13,910 --> 01:07:14,960
can't evaluate it.

1339
01:07:14,960 --> 01:07:16,460
But in real data
sets, it often gets

1340
01:07:16,460 --> 01:07:18,650
really hard to understand
what does it mean

1341
01:07:18,650 --> 01:07:21,772
to have sufficient coverage?

1342
01:07:21,772 --> 01:07:24,230
So in general, this is going
to be hard because we're going

1343
01:07:24,230 --> 01:07:26,420
to want to say, well, is it OK?

1344
01:07:26,420 --> 01:07:28,050
If it's 0, I
definitely can't do it.

1345
01:07:28,050 --> 01:07:30,500
But if it was like right
here, is that sufficient?

1346
01:07:30,500 --> 01:07:33,570
If like one in a million
times I use a vasopressor,

1347
01:07:33,570 --> 01:07:35,040
is that going to be OK?

1348
01:07:35,040 --> 01:07:37,010
Does it have to be in
my actual data set,

1349
01:07:37,010 --> 01:07:40,070
or does it just have to be there
was a chance of me doing this?

1350
01:07:40,070 --> 01:07:42,560
So all these issues are
kind of exactly how much

1351
01:07:42,560 --> 01:07:45,810
data support you need come up.

1352
01:07:45,810 --> 01:07:50,260
So up to around 2020,
most of the methods

1353
01:07:50,260 --> 01:07:52,810
for doing off-policy
evaluation, kind of model based

1354
01:07:52,810 --> 01:07:55,000
or model free, assumed overlap.

1355
01:07:55,000 --> 01:07:58,100
So if you're doing
off-policy estimation,

1356
01:07:58,100 --> 01:07:59,690
it means for your
policy of interest.

1357
01:07:59,690 --> 01:08:03,560
But for off-policy optimization,
it often assumed all policies.

1358
01:08:03,560 --> 01:08:06,850
So every single policy you
can imagine in your domain

1359
01:08:06,850 --> 01:08:10,000
had to have coverage with
your behavior policy.

1360
01:08:10,000 --> 01:08:13,240
Now, if your behavior policy
is random, that's fine.

1361
01:08:13,240 --> 01:08:16,720
But if your behavior policy
is, say, how physicians operate

1362
01:08:16,720 --> 01:08:19,390
or how teachers operate
or some sort of policy

1363
01:08:19,390 --> 01:08:21,729
that's not completely
random, that

1364
01:08:21,729 --> 01:08:24,729
wouldn't always be satisfied.

1365
01:08:24,729 --> 01:08:26,910
And in general,
many, many real data

1366
01:08:26,910 --> 01:08:30,100
sets don't involve complete
random exploration.

1367
01:08:30,100 --> 01:08:33,069
And this means if you assume
this and use these methods

1368
01:08:33,069 --> 01:08:35,350
and it's not true,
then you might end up

1369
01:08:35,350 --> 01:08:37,450
sort of going into
parts of the domain

1370
01:08:37,450 --> 01:08:40,330
or ending up taking policies
that go into parts of the domain

1371
01:08:40,330 --> 01:08:41,870
where you have very
little coverage.

1372
01:08:45,240 --> 01:08:47,260
So I'm going to
introduce an idea.

1373
01:08:47,260 --> 01:08:49,630
And it turns out this idea,
there was a number of groups

1374
01:08:49,630 --> 01:08:51,880
that all started thinking
about this at the same time,

1375
01:08:51,880 --> 01:08:55,020
and I'll cite a few others
of them in a minute.

1376
01:08:55,020 --> 01:08:57,240
We call this doing the
best with what you've got.

1377
01:08:57,240 --> 01:09:00,479
So the idea was, how can we
leverage data sets where we only

1378
01:09:00,479 --> 01:09:02,287
have partial coverage?

1379
01:09:02,287 --> 01:09:03,870
Like we still want
to do as well as we

1380
01:09:03,870 --> 01:09:07,200
can, but within the
support of the data.

1381
01:09:07,200 --> 01:09:10,149
And this is similar to
the KL constraint or PPO

1382
01:09:10,149 --> 01:09:12,149
clipping that we've seen
before, but this is all

1383
01:09:12,149 --> 01:09:13,816
going to be entirely
in the offline case

1384
01:09:13,816 --> 01:09:16,200
where we don't manage to
get any additional data.

1385
01:09:16,200 --> 01:09:18,450
And the key idea that we're
going to think about here

1386
01:09:18,450 --> 01:09:21,359
is just being
pessimistic so that when

1387
01:09:21,359 --> 01:09:23,189
we don't think we have
sufficient coverage,

1388
01:09:23,189 --> 01:09:26,370
or we have high uncertainty
over what the reward might

1389
01:09:26,370 --> 01:09:28,330
be in a particular
state or action,

1390
01:09:28,330 --> 01:09:32,510
we want to be pessimistic with
respect to that uncertainty.

1391
01:09:32,510 --> 01:09:35,060
I want to highlight that just
even when our paper came out

1392
01:09:35,060 --> 01:09:36,770
here, there was
sort of increasing

1393
01:09:36,770 --> 01:09:38,910
interest in offline RL.

1394
01:09:38,910 --> 01:09:41,180
But what we noted is
that there was still

1395
01:09:41,180 --> 01:09:42,649
quite a few
challenges, and I just

1396
01:09:42,649 --> 01:09:45,380
want to illustrate that with
a really simple example.

1397
01:09:45,380 --> 01:09:47,138
So this is known
as the chain MDP,

1398
01:09:47,138 --> 01:09:49,430
and we might talk about it
more when we talk about data

1399
01:09:49,430 --> 01:09:51,649
efficient exploration.

1400
01:09:51,649 --> 01:09:54,822
This is not exactly the
same as all the chain MDPs,

1401
01:09:54,822 --> 01:09:56,030
but there's a number of them.

1402
01:09:56,030 --> 01:09:58,130
And they're used to
illustrate the hardness

1403
01:09:58,130 --> 01:10:00,380
of learning good policies.

1404
01:10:00,380 --> 01:10:02,360
So the idea in
this setting is you

1405
01:10:02,360 --> 01:10:06,260
have an initial start
state S1 and then--

1406
01:10:06,260 --> 01:10:07,550
or sorry, S0.

1407
01:10:07,550 --> 01:10:10,310
And then under one
policy, mu, you

1408
01:10:10,310 --> 01:10:16,250
have a probability of
going to S1, S2, et cetera.

1409
01:10:16,250 --> 01:10:18,420
And also with
another probability,

1410
01:10:18,420 --> 01:10:21,040
you have a probability
of transitioning to S10.

1411
01:10:21,040 --> 01:10:24,080
It's a really, really small
MDP, just a very small number

1412
01:10:24,080 --> 01:10:25,280
of states.

1413
01:10:25,280 --> 01:10:29,360
The important thing to note
here is that all of these states

1414
01:10:29,360 --> 01:10:33,900
have deterministic reward
except for this one.

1415
01:10:33,900 --> 01:10:40,560
So in reality, this has
an expected reward of 0.8.

1416
01:10:40,560 --> 01:10:42,760
You always get 0.8 when
you get to that state.

1417
01:10:42,760 --> 01:10:46,710
And this one has an
expected reward of 0.5.

1418
01:10:46,710 --> 01:10:48,990
So it's a worse state.

1419
01:10:48,990 --> 01:10:52,150
But if you go there because of
stochasticity, some of the time,

1420
01:10:52,150 --> 01:10:54,910
you'll get a 1 there, which
means when you have finite data,

1421
01:10:54,910 --> 01:10:57,960
you might think that state
s9 is better than S10.

1422
01:10:57,960 --> 01:11:00,930
That will just happen
with your data.

1423
01:11:00,930 --> 01:11:03,060
So what we could
show in this case

1424
01:11:03,060 --> 01:11:05,760
is that a bunch of the
other algorithms for doing

1425
01:11:05,760 --> 01:11:06,718
conservative batch RL--

1426
01:11:06,718 --> 01:11:09,260
and I won't go through all of
them, but happy to talk to them

1427
01:11:09,260 --> 01:11:09,840
offline--

1428
01:11:09,840 --> 01:11:14,250
had this weird behavior where as
you this is your behavior data

1429
01:11:14,250 --> 01:11:14,960
set.

1430
01:11:14,960 --> 01:11:17,560
So as you increase the
amount of your behavior data,

1431
01:11:17,560 --> 01:11:18,990
you would hope in
general that you

1432
01:11:18,990 --> 01:11:21,240
get a better, better
estimate of a new policy,

1433
01:11:21,240 --> 01:11:23,660
and you get actually better
and better performance.

1434
01:11:23,660 --> 01:11:26,100
This is the success rate.

1435
01:11:26,100 --> 01:11:28,620
And we found we had this
weird behavior where

1436
01:11:28,620 --> 01:11:30,750
for a lot of the
other algorithms,

1437
01:11:30,750 --> 01:11:32,250
they would start
off, and they would

1438
01:11:32,250 --> 01:11:34,440
learn the optimal policy
for this domain, which

1439
01:11:34,440 --> 01:11:36,510
is to go to S10.

1440
01:11:36,510 --> 01:11:40,420
But then as you got more data
from your behavior data set,

1441
01:11:40,420 --> 01:11:43,770
they would get misled because
sometime you would have seen S9,

1442
01:11:43,770 --> 01:11:45,900
and it would have
given you a one.

1443
01:11:45,900 --> 01:11:48,550
And if it saw that, it would say
no, I don't want to go to S10.

1444
01:11:48,550 --> 01:11:50,140
I want to go to S9 instead.

1445
01:11:50,140 --> 01:11:52,210
So with intermediate
amounts of data,

1446
01:11:52,210 --> 01:11:53,820
these other methods
would get confused

1447
01:11:53,820 --> 01:11:55,005
and learned a bad policy.

1448
01:11:55,005 --> 01:11:57,630
And it was only as you started
to get a lot more data that they

1449
01:11:57,630 --> 01:11:59,580
would end up getting
back and realizing

1450
01:11:59,580 --> 01:12:01,780
what the best policy was.

1451
01:12:01,780 --> 01:12:03,350
And so that was
somewhat concerning,

1452
01:12:03,350 --> 01:12:05,590
because you would
generally hope that you

1453
01:12:05,590 --> 01:12:07,660
get some sort of
monotonic improvement

1454
01:12:07,660 --> 01:12:10,990
as you get more and more data
from your behavior data set.

1455
01:12:10,990 --> 01:12:13,720
But here we were seeing that
some of the previous methods

1456
01:12:13,720 --> 01:12:17,350
had this sort of
unfortunate behavior.

1457
01:12:17,350 --> 01:12:19,420
And it turned out it
didn't just happen in

1458
01:12:19,420 --> 01:12:21,370
for these particular
examples, but we

1459
01:12:21,370 --> 01:12:23,650
could show some other
types of examples

1460
01:12:23,650 --> 01:12:26,380
where we got very similar
types of performance challenges

1461
01:12:26,380 --> 01:12:28,480
for other methods.

1462
01:12:28,480 --> 01:12:32,810
So the key idea
is pretty simple,

1463
01:12:32,810 --> 01:12:35,740
which is just be pessimistic
if you haven't seen a state

1464
01:12:35,740 --> 01:12:37,360
action very much.

1465
01:12:37,360 --> 01:12:40,510
So we defined a filtration
function, which is just

1466
01:12:40,510 --> 01:12:43,240
a simple threshold
that says, let me check

1467
01:12:43,240 --> 01:12:44,730
for this state and action pair.

1468
01:12:44,730 --> 01:12:47,210
It's kind of what my density
is, how much I've seen it.

1469
01:12:47,210 --> 01:12:48,800
If it's greater
than a threshold,

1470
01:12:48,800 --> 01:12:51,400
this is going to be 1.

1471
01:12:51,400 --> 01:12:54,067
OK, so what this
threshold is doing

1472
01:12:54,067 --> 01:12:56,650
is trying to account for your
statistical uncertainty you have

1473
01:12:56,650 --> 01:12:58,533
if you have finite
amounts of data.

1474
01:12:58,533 --> 01:13:00,200
So if you haven't
seen things very much,

1475
01:13:00,200 --> 01:13:01,810
this is going to become 0.

1476
01:13:01,810 --> 01:13:03,940
If you've seen things a
lot, it's going to be one.

1477
01:13:03,940 --> 01:13:05,980
That's all we're doing.

1478
01:13:05,980 --> 01:13:08,750
And then we can just combine
this with Bellman backups.

1479
01:13:08,750 --> 01:13:12,770
So just like for DQN or Belmont
operator, we can just apply it.

1480
01:13:12,770 --> 01:13:14,830
So that when we are
looking at your reward

1481
01:13:14,830 --> 01:13:18,020
plus gamma times your expected
discounted sum of rewards,

1482
01:13:18,020 --> 01:13:20,480
we look at the states
you might get into.

1483
01:13:20,480 --> 01:13:23,270
And if those states we don't
have very much data for,

1484
01:13:23,270 --> 01:13:25,900
then this whole thing becomes 0.

1485
01:13:25,900 --> 01:13:28,810
And so it's like
saying if I transition

1486
01:13:28,810 --> 01:13:31,670
to a next state for which
I don't have much data,

1487
01:13:31,670 --> 01:13:33,380
I just pretend its reward is 0.

1488
01:13:33,380 --> 01:13:36,340
And then I back up from
there, which essentially

1489
01:13:36,340 --> 01:13:38,080
means I don't want
to take actions

1490
01:13:38,080 --> 01:13:41,970
that transition to states for
which I don't have enough data--

1491
01:13:41,970 --> 01:13:43,770
just pessimistic.

1492
01:13:43,770 --> 01:13:46,798
And it's going to
be a lower bound.

1493
01:13:46,798 --> 01:13:48,340
If your rewards are
all bounded by 0,

1494
01:13:48,340 --> 01:13:52,430
it's just going to be a lower
bound on your potential reward.

1495
01:13:52,430 --> 01:13:56,000
So since we assume that our
rewards are all positive

1496
01:13:56,000 --> 01:13:57,470
and you can always
just shift them,

1497
01:13:57,470 --> 01:13:59,775
this is going to become
a pessimistic estimate

1498
01:13:59,775 --> 01:14:00,775
for all of those tuples.

1499
01:14:03,970 --> 01:14:08,020
And you can do this for either
policy evaluation or for--

1500
01:14:08,020 --> 01:14:10,570
so you can use this in like
policy gradient type approaches

1501
01:14:10,570 --> 01:14:14,870
or for Q-learning type methods.

1502
01:14:14,870 --> 01:14:17,090
And it turns out
this helps a lot.

1503
01:14:17,090 --> 01:14:20,540
So let me just-- we call this
marginalized behavior supported

1504
01:14:20,540 --> 01:14:22,730
policy optimization.

1505
01:14:22,730 --> 01:14:24,328
I'll just highlight what was--

1506
01:14:24,328 --> 01:14:26,120
because one of the key
things of this paper

1507
01:14:26,120 --> 01:14:28,310
was the theory that
we showed with it.

1508
01:14:28,310 --> 01:14:30,200
As I said, a lot of
the previous methods

1509
01:14:30,200 --> 01:14:34,770
had to make assumptions over
coverage that like your data,

1510
01:14:34,770 --> 01:14:38,070
covered any possible policy
that you might want to evaluate.

1511
01:14:38,070 --> 01:14:42,260
And under that, you could ensure
that the policy that you learn

1512
01:14:42,260 --> 01:14:44,840
is close to optimal.

1513
01:14:44,840 --> 01:14:46,680
Ours does not make
that guarantee.

1514
01:14:46,680 --> 01:14:50,060
It only says, let's
think about all policies

1515
01:14:50,060 --> 01:14:52,400
that we could reasonably
evaluate that have

1516
01:14:52,400 --> 01:14:53,790
sort of enough coverage.

1517
01:14:53,790 --> 01:14:57,025
We are guaranteed to find the
best policy within that class.

1518
01:14:59,980 --> 01:15:02,110
I'll skip through
this now due to time.

1519
01:15:02,110 --> 01:15:04,630
But under some assumptions
that we can also

1520
01:15:04,630 --> 01:15:07,350
give these kind of
finite sample guarantees

1521
01:15:07,350 --> 01:15:12,090
similar to what we saw for
the fitted Q evaluation.

1522
01:15:12,090 --> 01:15:12,750
All right.

1523
01:15:12,750 --> 01:15:15,210
And I'll just highlight that
those do include the function

1524
01:15:15,210 --> 01:15:16,480
approximation.

1525
01:15:16,480 --> 01:15:18,760
So these aren't for tabular.

1526
01:15:18,760 --> 01:15:21,340
So this is what's
pretty cool to see.

1527
01:15:21,340 --> 01:15:23,490
So in this case, this is hopper.

1528
01:15:23,490 --> 01:15:24,910
This is the behavior policy.

1529
01:15:24,910 --> 01:15:27,490
So this is the behavior policy
used to gather the data.

1530
01:15:27,490 --> 01:15:32,490
What you can see here is if you
use DDPG, that actually does

1531
01:15:32,490 --> 01:15:34,740
worse than the behavior policy.

1532
01:15:34,740 --> 01:15:37,920
If you use behavior cloning,
it was a little bit better,

1533
01:15:37,920 --> 01:15:38,620
about the same.

1534
01:15:38,620 --> 01:15:40,590
It used a particular Vi.

1535
01:15:40,590 --> 01:15:44,580
We then compare it to BCQ, which
I mentioned briefly before,

1536
01:15:44,580 --> 01:15:48,360
Scott Fujimoto's work, and you
can see that and our approach

1537
01:15:48,360 --> 01:15:50,670
in green both do
substantially better.

1538
01:15:50,670 --> 01:15:52,420
Again, highly in
some of these cases,

1539
01:15:52,420 --> 01:15:55,020
the data does support you
learning a much better policy,

1540
01:15:55,020 --> 01:15:57,840
and you should do-- you
should try to uncover that

1541
01:15:57,840 --> 01:15:59,790
by using these methods
that explicitly

1542
01:15:59,790 --> 01:16:01,240
think about your uncertainty.

1543
01:16:03,810 --> 01:16:06,350
Now, I'll skip this
just due to time.

1544
01:16:06,350 --> 01:16:08,730
There's some interesting
theoretical reasons

1545
01:16:08,730 --> 01:16:11,250
why model based
might be even better.

1546
01:16:11,250 --> 01:16:13,992
At the same time,
there were three papers

1547
01:16:13,992 --> 01:16:16,200
that all came out at NeurIPS--
ours was one of them--

1548
01:16:16,200 --> 01:16:18,940
the same year with all
basically very related ideas.

1549
01:16:18,940 --> 01:16:22,140
Ours was a model-free
based approach and work

1550
01:16:22,140 --> 01:16:24,180
by some of my
colleagues, Chelsea Finn,

1551
01:16:24,180 --> 01:16:28,500
and [INAUDIBLE] and others,
learned a model-based approach

1552
01:16:28,500 --> 01:16:31,900
where they penalize model
uncertainty during planning.

1553
01:16:31,900 --> 01:16:34,310
And they had some very
nice results in D4RL cases.

1554
01:16:34,310 --> 01:16:36,390
Ours is a bit more
theoretical and model free.

1555
01:16:36,390 --> 01:16:39,700
Theirs was a little more
algorithmic and empirical

1556
01:16:39,700 --> 01:16:41,650
and also had some really nice--

1557
01:16:41,650 --> 01:16:45,100
and was focused on
model-based approaches.

1558
01:16:45,100 --> 01:16:48,340
I'll just highlight that another
method that came out similarly

1559
01:16:48,340 --> 01:16:51,050
around the same time was
conservative Q-learning,

1560
01:16:51,050 --> 01:16:54,370
and that has also continued
to be very popular since.

1561
01:16:54,370 --> 01:16:58,922
So that's another way to think
about being conservative.

1562
01:16:58,922 --> 01:17:00,380
We're almost out
of time, so I just

1563
01:17:00,380 --> 01:17:05,680
want to do share how do these
different approaches compare.

1564
01:17:05,680 --> 01:17:07,960
Pessimistic
approaches in general

1565
01:17:07,960 --> 01:17:10,250
do better than alternatives.

1566
01:17:10,250 --> 01:17:13,150
All of these have some
form of pessimism.

1567
01:17:13,150 --> 01:17:14,960
These are model based.

1568
01:17:14,960 --> 01:17:18,220
This is the sort of behavior
constrained Q-learning,

1569
01:17:18,220 --> 01:17:21,190
some nice work there from
Sergey Levine's group

1570
01:17:21,190 --> 01:17:25,960
from Berkeley and CQL,
which is also from Berkeley.

1571
01:17:25,960 --> 01:17:28,180
The different methods
tend to do better or worse

1572
01:17:28,180 --> 01:17:30,940
in different settings.

1573
01:17:30,940 --> 01:17:33,760
I think that in
general, the key thing

1574
01:17:33,760 --> 01:17:35,740
to understand from this
part is that it really

1575
01:17:35,740 --> 01:17:38,860
can be beneficial to think
explicitly about uncertainty

1576
01:17:38,860 --> 01:17:41,530
and use that to
penalize and constrain

1577
01:17:41,530 --> 01:17:44,290
your function to be in
the parts of the domain

1578
01:17:44,290 --> 01:17:46,660
where you have support.

1579
01:17:46,660 --> 01:17:49,070
And again, this
is pretty similar.

1580
01:17:49,070 --> 01:17:54,800
It should definitely make
you think back to PPO

1581
01:17:54,800 --> 01:18:02,040
instead of having
constrained updates.

1582
01:18:02,040 --> 01:18:03,700
So many of these
different settings--

1583
01:18:03,700 --> 01:18:07,620
we're really trying to think
explicitly about coverage

1584
01:18:07,620 --> 01:18:10,630
and how far we can use
the existing data we have.

1585
01:18:10,630 --> 01:18:12,273
But particularly,
here where we assume

1586
01:18:12,273 --> 01:18:13,690
you don't get any
additional data,

1587
01:18:13,690 --> 01:18:15,690
you're just going to
deploy a policy at the end,

1588
01:18:15,690 --> 01:18:20,680
we want to think about exactly
how much support we have.

1589
01:18:20,680 --> 01:18:22,505
OK all right.

1590
01:18:22,505 --> 01:18:24,130
I will skip the last
part because we're

1591
01:18:24,130 --> 01:18:25,130
going to be out of time.

1592
01:18:25,130 --> 01:18:26,710
If you're interested,
I just want

1593
01:18:26,710 --> 01:18:30,010
to highlight that you can
extend these ideas to think

1594
01:18:30,010 --> 01:18:32,120
about there being constraints.

1595
01:18:32,120 --> 01:18:34,420
So we had a science paper
a few years ago thinking

1596
01:18:34,420 --> 01:18:35,878
about what if you
want to make sure

1597
01:18:35,878 --> 01:18:38,590
your performance is improving
compared to baselines.

1598
01:18:38,590 --> 01:18:43,300
And in particular, we used like
a diabetes insulin measurement

1599
01:18:43,300 --> 01:18:44,082
simulator.

1600
01:18:44,082 --> 01:18:45,290
It's a really cool simulator.

1601
01:18:45,290 --> 01:18:47,950
It was approved by the FDA
to replace early-stage animal

1602
01:18:47,950 --> 01:18:48,530
trials.

1603
01:18:48,530 --> 01:18:52,340
And you can learn new ways
to do insulin delivery.

1604
01:18:52,340 --> 01:18:54,580
And what we wanted to
illustrate in this case

1605
01:18:54,580 --> 01:18:57,940
is that by thinking explicitly
about your uncertainty

1606
01:18:57,940 --> 01:19:00,320
over the performance of
new decision policies,

1607
01:19:00,320 --> 01:19:02,530
you could quickly
learn a policy that you

1608
01:19:02,530 --> 01:19:05,940
were confident would be better
than the existing policy.

1609
01:19:05,940 --> 01:19:07,690
So I just highlight
that to say that there

1610
01:19:07,690 --> 01:19:10,030
are lots of cases where you'd
like to do this offline policy

1611
01:19:10,030 --> 01:19:11,447
learning, but do
so in a way where

1612
01:19:11,447 --> 01:19:13,420
you have safety
constraints or constraints

1613
01:19:13,420 --> 01:19:15,730
over the performance.

1614
01:19:15,730 --> 01:19:16,230
All right.

1615
01:19:16,230 --> 01:19:17,850
Let me just summarize this part.

1616
01:19:17,850 --> 01:19:21,680
So in terms of things that you
should know or be able to do--

1617
01:19:21,680 --> 01:19:22,180
excuse me.

1618
01:19:22,180 --> 01:19:23,580
You should be able to
define and apply importance

1619
01:19:23,580 --> 01:19:25,627
sampling for off policy,
policy evaluation

1620
01:19:25,627 --> 01:19:27,210
and understand some
of the limitations

1621
01:19:27,210 --> 01:19:29,550
of these prior works.

1622
01:19:29,550 --> 01:19:31,380
You should understand
why offline RL

1623
01:19:31,380 --> 01:19:33,940
might be able to outperform
imitation learning.

1624
01:19:33,940 --> 01:19:36,630
You should know this idea of
pessimism under uncertainty

1625
01:19:36,630 --> 01:19:39,390
and be able to have some
application areas where

1626
01:19:39,390 --> 01:19:42,540
you might want to be doing
offline RL or offline policy

1627
01:19:42,540 --> 01:19:43,450
evaluation.

1628
01:19:43,450 --> 01:19:46,410
So particularly in high-risk
settings, that can be important.

1629
01:19:46,410 --> 01:19:49,980
What we'll be doing next is
going to start to talk about how

1630
01:19:49,980 --> 01:19:51,842
if we can gather
our data, how we

1631
01:19:51,842 --> 01:19:54,300
should gather our data in order
to really efficiently learn

1632
01:19:54,300 --> 01:19:55,270
policies?

1633
